#####################################################################
# SFS Lab: Writing a Multithreaded File System
#
# Developer-edition student handout files
#####################################################################

For a full description of the lab, see the writeup (sfslab.pdf),
which is extracted alongside the sfslab/ directory.

This branch provides the unscored D/X extensions.  It deliberately leaves
sfs_getpos, sfs_seek, sfs_rename, and student synchronization unfinished;
use main instead when you want the course-shaped starter semantics.

***********
Main files:
***********

sfs-disk.c
        The SFS implementation.  This is the only file you need to
        edit.  It contains three unimplemented functions (sfs_getpos,
        sfs_seek, sfs_rename) and, once those work, it is where you
        add locking to make the file system thread-safe and scalable.

sfs-api.h
        The API specification you are implementing.  Read the doc
        comment above each function carefully.  Do not modify.

test-sfs.c
        The test driver and autograder.  Type "make test" (or run
        ./test-sfs) to score your work.

**********************************
Other support files for the driver
**********************************

sfs-disk.h          On-disk data structures and constants.
sfs-support.c       Low-level disk image and mmap support.
sfs-fsck.c          File system consistency checker (./sfs-fsck).
sfs-baseline-ref.c  Handout code under one global mutex; used by
                    "make baseline" to calibrate the performance
                    score for your machine.  Do not modify.
Makefile            Builds sfs-fsck, test-sfs, and test-sfs-baseline.
.clang-format       Formatting configuration matching the handout style.

*******************************
Building and running the driver
*******************************

To build the driver, type:

    unix> make

To run the autograder:

    unix> ./test-sfs

To run the developer-only checks (not scored):

    unix> make developer-test
    unix> make x-traces

The developer starter is expected to fail some traces: sfs_getpos,
sfs_seek, and sfs_rename are intentionally left for you to implement.

Before reading anything into the performance score, calibrate the
baseline for your machine (once per machine, and again after the
machine changes):

    unix> make baseline

"make grade" runs the calibration and the autograder in one step.
Run everything from this directory; the driver expects ./sfs-fsck
and ./.perf_baseline to be reachable from the working directory.

The developer checks cover sfs_fstat/sfs_ftruncate and the zero-block empty
file, expandable-directory, and Unix-unlink routes.  They do not affect the
22-point score.
